# Practical 5: Cloud Storage with Supabase


## What I Did

In this practical I migrated the TikTok clone from storing uploaded videos and thumbnails locally on the server to storing them in Supabase Storage — a cloud-based file storage service. Previously every uploaded file was saved to an `uploads/` folder on the Express server, which has serious limitations in a real application. After this practical, all media files are stored in Supabase Storage buckets, delivered through a global CDN, and the server's disk is no longer involved in file storage at all.

---

## Tech Stack Added

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Supabase Storage | Cloud bucket storage for videos and thumbnails |
| `@supabase/supabase-js` | JavaScript SDK for interacting with Supabase |
| Multer (memory storage) | Changed from disk storage to memory buffer for cloud upload |

---

## What Changed

### Backend
- Created `src/lib/supabase.js` — Supabase client using the service role key
- Created `src/services/storageService.js` — `uploadFile` and `deleteFile` functions
- Updated `videoController.js` — uploads now go to Supabase, URLs saved to DB
- Updated `videoRoutes.js` — Multer switched from disk to memory storage
- Updated `prisma/schema.prisma` — added `videoStoragePath` and `thumbnailStoragePath` fields
- Ran migration: `npx prisma migrate dev --name add_storage_paths`

### Frontend
- Installed `@supabase/supabase-js`
- Created `src/lib/supabase.js` — frontend Supabase client
- Updated `src/app/upload/page.jsx` — added upload progress indicator

